Drop ExprPath's counted reference to its source accessor.

A parsed path literal is an ExprPath, which held `ref<SourceAccessor>
accessor` - usually `state->rootFS`. Exprs::add places expressions in a
std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource, which frees memory without running
destructors, so that reference is never dropped. rootFS mounts storeFS,
which mounts `store->getFSAccessor()`, which holds
`ref<Store>(shared_from_this())`. So once an evaluator has parsed one path
literal, its Store and the Store's daemon connections outlive it for the
life of the process. The Nix CLI exits after one evaluation and never sees
this; an embedder that opens many evaluators runs the daemon out of file
descriptors (huggorm#128). Measured: one leaked daemon socket per closed
evaluator that ran `builtins.path { path = /abs; }`; gdb shows ~EvalState
and never ~Store.

Nothing reads the member. `v` already holds the accessor as a raw pointer,
as every path Value does, and every accessor a parsed path names is owned
by the EvalState that owns the parsed expressions.

--- a/src/libexpr/include/nix/expr/nixexpr.hh	2026-10-06 11:12:15.421117989 +0200
+++ b/src/libexpr/include/nix/expr/nixexpr.hh	2026-10-06 11:12:38.550276866 +0200
@@ -208,11 +208,9 @@
 
 struct ExprPath : Expr
 {
-    ref<SourceAccessor> accessor;
     Value v;
 
     ExprPath(std::pmr::polymorphic_allocator<char> & alloc, ref<SourceAccessor> accessor, std::string_view sv)
-        : accessor(accessor)
     {
         v.mkPath(&*accessor, StringData::make(*alloc.resource(), sv));
     }
